## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key insights show that a 67% salary increase (from $150,000 to $250,000) for the Miami-Dade County Mayor position was approved in the county's 2018 budget. However, this approval was made by the county commission, not by Carlos Gimenez personally voting on or formally approving his own raise. Furthermore, the raise did not directly enact a separate pension increase; instead, the higher salary automatically improved his pension benefit calculations under the Florida Retirement System formula by increasing his high-5 average salary.

The evidence does not support the claim that Carlos Gimenez *himself* approved the raise. The claim's wording implies a direct personal action, whereas the raise was part of a budget passed by the legislative body (the county commission). The connection to his pension is presented as an indirect consequence of the salary increase, not as a separate action he took to increase it.

Therefore, the claim is not fully supported by the evidence as it misattributes the approval action. The final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The salary increase for Miami-Dade's mayor was [approved by the county commission](https://www.politicalcortadito.com/2018/09/21/salary-raise-mayor-carlos-gimenez/) in 2018, not by Gimenez personally voting on his own raise. The potential 67% increase (from $150,000 to $250,000) was authorized in the commission's budget. Furthermore, [any resulting boost to his pension](https://www.politicalcortadito.com/2018/09/22/3-reasons-why-carlos-gimenez-will-jack-his-salary-pension-suarez-mo-money/) was an automatic consequence of the higher salary under the Florida Retirement System's formula, not a separate legislative action he enacted.